##1st docker

Step 1: Install Docker
Download and install Docker Desktop from the official Docker website. After installation, start Docker 
Desktop and ensure the Docker service is running.
Step 2: Verify Docker Installation
Open Command Prompt, PowerShell, or Terminal and execute: docker --version A successful 
installation displays the installed Docker version.
Step 3: Verify Docker Service 
Check whether Docker is functioning properly: docker info This command displays Docker Engine 
information and confirms communication with the Docker daemon.
Step 4: Download an Image 
Pull the Ubuntu image from Docker Hub: docker pull ubuntu Docker downloads the required image 
layers and stores them locally.
Step 5: View Available Images 
Display all downloaded images: docker images The command shows: • Repository name • Tag • Image 
ID • Creation date • Image size
Step 6: Create and Run a Container 
Create and start a container using the Ubuntu image: docker run -it ubuntu bash Explanation: • -i : 
Interactive mode. • -t : Allocates a terminal. • ubuntu : Image name. • bash : Starts the Bash shell. The 
terminal prompt changes to indicate execution inside the container. 
Step 7: Interact with the Container 
Execute commands within the running container: 
 pwd 
 ls 
 whoami 
These commands demonstrate interaction with the containerized environment
Step 8: Exit the Container 
Exit the running container: exit The container stops after exiting the shell session.
Step 9: View Container Lifecycle Status 
Display all containers: docker ps -a 
Observe the container status: • Up (Running) • Exited (Stopped) 
This demonstrates lifecycle state transitions.
Step 10: Restart the Container 
Start the stopped container again: docker start <container_id>
Verify the status: docker ps
Step 11: Access the Running Container 
Connect to the container: docker exec -it bash This opens an interactive shell session within the 
container.
Step 12: Remove the Container
Stop the container if necessary: docker stop Remove it: docker rm This deletes the container from the 
Docker host.
Step 13: Remove the Image 
Delete the image: docker rmi ubuntu This removes the image from local storage


