##3rd kubernetes

Step 1: Start the Kubernetes cluster
Start Minikube and verify that the cluster is running. minikube start --driver=docker kubectl get nodes 
Step 2: Create the application
Create a project directory and an index.html file containing a simple webpage with the message “Hello 
from Kubernetes!”. 
Step 3: Create the Dockerfile
Create a file named Dockerfile with the following instructions: 
FROM nginx:alpine 
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html 
EXPOSE 80 
Step 4: Build the Docker image
Build the application image using the following command: 
docker build -t k8s-demo:v1 
Step 5: Load the image into Minikube
Make the Docker image available to the Kubernetes cluster. 
minikube image load k8s-demo:v1 
Step 6: Create a Kubernetes Deployment
Create a Deployment with two replicas of the application. 
kubectl create deployment k8s-demo --image=k8s-demo:v1 --replicas=2 
Verify the Deployment: kubectl get deployments 
Step 7: Verify the Pods
Check whether both application Pods are running. 
kubectl get pods 
Both Pods should show Running status and 1/1 readiness. 
Step 8: Expose the application
Create a NodePort Service to expose the application on port 80. 
kubectl expose deployment k8s-demo --type=NodePort --port=80 
Verify the Service: 
kubectl get services
Step 9: Access the application
Run the following command to access the application through a browser: 
minikube service k8s-demo 
The webpage displays the message “Hello from Kubernetes!”, confirming that the application is 
accessible.

##4th puppet pull based

Step 1: Update the package repositories 
Update the Ubuntu package lists before installing Puppet. 
sudo apt update 
Step 2: Install Puppet Agent
Install the Puppet Agent package. 
sudo apt install puppet-agent 
Verify the installation: 
puppet --version 
Step 3: Install Puppet Server
Install the Puppet Server package. 
sudo apt install puppetserver
Step 4: Start and verify the Puppet Server
Start the Puppet Server service. 
sudo systemctl start puppetserver 
Check its status: 
sudo systemctl status puppetserver 
The service should display active (running). 
Step 5: Configure the Puppet Server hostname
Check the server hostname configured for the agent. 
sudo puppet config print server --section agent 
The configured hostname is puppet. Add the hostname mapping to /etc/hosts so the agent can resolve it 
locally. 
echo "127.0.0.1 puppet" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts 
Verify the hostname resolution: 
getent hosts puppet 
Step 6: Verify the Puppet Server port
Check whether the Puppet Server is listening on port 8140
sudo ss -ltnp | grep 8140 
The output should show a listening service on port 8140. 
Step 7: Create a Puppet manifest
Create a manifest named first.pp. 
nano first.pp 
Enter the following Puppet code: 
file { '/tmp/puppet-demo': ensure => directory, } 
Save the file and exit the editor. 
Step 8: Apply the Puppet manifest
Execute the manifest using the Puppet command. 
sudo puppet apply first.pp
Step 9: Verify the resource
Check whether the directory has been created. 
ls -ld /tmp/puppet-demo 
The output should display the directory and its permissions. 
Step 10: Verify idempotency
Apply the same manifest again. 
sudo puppet apply first.pp 
Since the directory already exists in the desired state, Puppet should report that no resource changes are 
required.
Step 11: Execute the Puppet Agent
Run the Puppet Agent in test mode to initiate communication with the Puppet Server. 
sudo puppet agent --test


##5th puppet blocks

Step 1: Create the project directory 
Create a directory for the Puppet experiment and move into it. 
mkdir -p ~/puppet-blocks-lab/modules/webdemo/manifests 
cd ~/puppet-blocks-lab
Step 2: Create a Puppet module 
A module organizes related configuration code. The webdemo module contains a manifests directory 
and an init.pp file.
Step 3: Create a class inside the module 
Create the module's main manifest. 
nano modules/webdemo/manifests/init.pp 
Add the following code: 
class webdemo {
file { '/tmp/puppet-blocks-demo.txt': 
ensure => file, 
content => upcase('puppet blocks demonstration'), } } 
Save the file and exit the editor. Here, class webdemo defines a class, while the file resource ensures 
that the specified file exists with the required content.
Step 4: Understand the function 
The built-in Puppet function upcase() converts a string into uppercase letters. For example: 
upcase('puppet blocks demonstration') 
The result is: 
PUPPET BLOCKS DEMONSTRATION 
This result is assigned to the file's content attribute. 
Step 5: Create the main manifest 
Create the main manifest that includes the module's class. 
nano site.pp 
Add the following code: 
include webdemo 
Save the file and exit the editor. The include statement declares the class so Puppet can apply the 
resources defined inside it. 
Step 6: Apply the Puppet configuration
Run Puppet with the module path specified. 
sudo puppet apply --modulepath=modules site.pp 
Puppet locates the webdemo module, evaluates its class, executes the function, and creates the 
configured file. 
Step 7: Verify the output
Display the generated file content. 
cat /tmp/puppet-blocks-demo.txt
Expected output: 
PUPPET BLOCKS DEMONSTRATION
